# XPdf — Sicil (Ottoman Court Register) Reader Fork

A fork of xpdf (v3.04, Glyph & Cog) for extracting text from PDFs of Ottoman
*sicil* (شريعة court register) scans, such as `data/24_Sicil_no.031.pdf`
(408 pages, produced by GPL Ghostscript 10.02.1 via `gs -sDEVICE=pdfwrite`).

## The problem

Two separate problems stand between these PDFs and usable text:

1. **The text is table data, and plain text extraction ignores the table
   structure.** These registers (and other structured documents, e.g.
   `data/sample-tables.pdf`) lay text out in ruled grids. Standard
   `pdftotext` only knows reading order, so a page with several
   independent tables stacked on it comes out as a single scrambled
   stream of words with no row/column boundaries.

2. **The Arabic font's `/ToUnicode` CMap is wrong.** The sicil body text
   uses embedded CID TrueType fonts (e.g. `YXKNPC+HuseyinGunday`) with
   `/Encoding /Identity-H`. The glyph *outlines* render correctly — the
   page looks perfect and a human can read it — but the `/ToUnicode`
   CMap that maps each CID to a Unicode codepoint is corrupt (a known
   Ghostscript `pdfwrite` subsetting defect). The most frequent
   Arabic-letter CIDs are mapped to ASCII space/digits/slash, and others
   to the wrong Arabic-block codepoints. Every extractor that trusts
   `/ToUnicode` — xpdf's own `pdftotext`, Poppler, MuPDF — emits garbage
   instead of the Arabic words. This can't be repaired from the
   `/ToUnicode` CMap itself, because the CMap *is* the wrong mapping;
   the correct CID→letter assignment has to come from somewhere else.

## Approach: two passes

### Pass 1 — `pdftoglyphs`: build a glyph database

`xpdf/pdftoglyphs` (a new tool, driven by a `SplashOutputDev` subclass,
`GlyphDbOutputDev`) walks every page of a document exactly as `pdftoppm`
would, but instead of writing page images, it captures one clean, isolated
bitmap per unique glyph — keyed by `(font, CID)`, **not** by the (broken)
Unicode value — the first time that glyph is seen. Because the glyph
shapes are correct even though their Unicode labels aren't, a human can
look at each captured bitmap and write down the correct letter, building a
`CID → Unicode` correction table that never has to consult `/ToUnicode` at
all.

The font key strips the random Ghostscript subset-tag prefix
(`YXKNPC+HuseyinGunday` → `HuseyinGunday`), since for an
Identity-mapped CID TrueType font the CID is tied to the *master* font's
glyph table, not to which subset of glyphs a given document happens to
embed. The intent is that one hand-labelled correction table, built once,
should be reusable across every register in the archive rather than
redone per document — this is still an assumption to validate as more
sicil documents are processed.

**Status:** glyph capture is implemented and working. Turning the
captured bitmaps into a labelled `CID → Unicode` table, and feeding that
table into pass 2 to actually correct the Arabic text, is not built yet.

### Pass 2 — `pdftotext -tablecells`: extract structured text

`xpdf/pdftotext -tablecells` uses another new `TextOutputDev` subclass,
`TableOutputDev`, which:

- watches `fill`/`eoFill`/`stroke` for thin filled rectangles or straight
  lines and records them as horizontal/vertical table-ruling segments,
  per page;
- clusters those rulings into row bands, and — independently *within
  each row band* (so that several differently-laid-out tables stacked on
  one page don't bleed into a single bogus grid) — into column
  boundaries;
- for each detected cell, calls `TextPage::getText()` on that cell's
  bounding box to pull its text in proper reading order (reusing xpdf's
  existing word/line clustering rather than reimplementing it), and
  emits `Row: r Col: c: <text>`; text above the first ruling is emitted
  as `Header:`, text below the last as `Footnotes:`; a page with no
  detected grid falls back to a single full-page `Text:` block.

**Status:** implemented and working (validated against
`data/sample-tables.pdf`). The `/ToUnicode` correction from pass 1 is not
yet wired in — running this against the sicil PDF today still produces
correctly-shaped table cells full of garbled Arabic, until pass 1's
output is joined in.

## Project layout

```
data/
  *.pdf          input documents (e.g. 24_Sicil_no.031.pdf, sample-tables.pdf)
  gylph_db/      pass 1 output: one <font>_<CIDhex>.ppm bitmap per unique glyph
  out/           pass 2 output: extracted text (one file per input document)
```

## Build

```bash
sudo apt-get install libmotif-dev libfreetype6-dev

./configure --with-freetype2-includes=/usr/include/freetype2

make
```

## Usage

```bash
# Pass 1: capture every unique glyph in a document into data/gylph_db/
xpdf/pdftoglyphs data/24_Sicil_no.031.pdf data/gylph_db

# Pass 2: extract table-structured text
xpdf/pdftotext -tablecells data/sample-tables.pdf data/out/sample-tables.txt
```

`pdftoglyphs` rasterizes glyphs at a fixed 900 DPI -- not user-configurable,
so the glyph-database md5 keys stay pixel-comparable across runs -- and
accepts `-f`/`-l` (page range) as well as the usual `-opw`/`-upw`/`-q`/`-cfg`.
`pdftotext`'s existing flags are unchanged; `-tablecells` is additive and doesn't affect
the default reading-order mode or the other layout modes (`-layout`,
`-table`, `-lineprinter`, `-raw`).
